<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Stall-Reader: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A read-only measure. Write nothing. Mark claims MEASURED (file:line) or INFER.

Worktree: /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/b1-lift/wt (branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5&#45;-b1-lift, head 0df40b07b; its code tree equals 55b631484, whose CI read GREEN in run 42256555201; the head adds .mem carriers only).

THE FAULT (CI run 42261057541, cell web-tw5; excerpts beside this brief: excerpt-1.log, excerpt-2.log): packages/lararium-web/tests/e2e/shore-writes.e2e.test.ts, two tests ("served wiki saves a draft tiddler without worker errors"; "the vessel page reports its instruments ...") time out in waitForLivePage (~:219) at page.waitForFunction 20000 ms. The page's #phases reads boot, repo-open, catalog-ready, bootstrap-kept, island-ready, corpus-ready, wiki-ready, vessel-ready, and faults= reads empty. So every boot milestone lands and the page never reaches "live". The same code read green once; the house law reads a recurrence as a fault, never a rerun, and never widens a timeout.

QUESTIONS, ranked:
# What sets the page "live" after vessel-ready (the exact condition waitForLivePage polls, and every product event that must arrive for it), in packages/lararium-web/src (main.ts, vessel-port.ts, floor-tab.ts, the projection sink, the surface/frames path) and packages/lararium-web/src/workers/vessel-host.ts.
# Which of b1-lift's slice-4 changes sit on that road (per-session surfaces: frames to every session of the lit face; the active-surface repaint for a joining tab; litFaceVessel's face-not-worn gate on set-surface / control-event / port:place / read:mirror-inputs; wearsLitFace; the late-layer replay) and can, by ORDER alone (no load needed), leave a page at vessel-ready without its first frame or live mark: e.g. the first frame sent before the session counts as wearing the lit face, a set-surface refused face-not-worn before the face lights, a repaint keyed to activeWikiId racing the first surface.
# Name the one order that reproduces it, the red that pins it on the product's own events (no sleep, no retry, no wider budget), and its control.
Final message under 800 words: line 1 names the most likely order in one sentence, MEASURED or INFER.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

